{"step_index":230,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-23T19:43:28Z","content":"I have fully implemented the requested upgrades for both the 3D viewer and the CAM Planner!\n\nHere is what I've updated in `index.html` and `app.js`:\n\n### 1. 3D Cinematic Camera & Graphics\n- **Perfect Fly-To**: I adjusted the `Cesium.HeadingPitchRange` to `(0, -30deg, 50km)`. Now, when you double-click or zoom to a satellite, the camera securely stops 50 kilometers out, tilting 30 degrees down to keep the 4K Earth majestically framed in the background without clipping through the object.\n- **HUD Reticles**: I removed the blurry default points and replaced them with `Cesium.BillboardGraphics` using an injected SVG data URI. Active satellites now render as glowing cyan crosshairs, and debris as red crosshairs!\n\n### 2. Advanced CAM Planner Features\n- **Propulsion Selector**: Added a sleek dropdown for \"Propulsion Type\" inside the CAM drawer.\n- **ISP Fuel Penalty**: The slider calculation is now dynamically linked to the propulsion type. Chemical (Hydrazine) accurately burns fuel fast ($3.5\\text{ kg}$ per $1.0\\text{ m/s}$), while toggling to Electric (Ion) drastically cuts the cost to $0.2\\text{ kg}$, perfectly demonstrating specific impulse efficiency!\n- **TCA Countdown Clock**: I built a prominent red digital clock at the top of the drawer. It grabs the UTC timestamp from the backend and runs a precise JavaScript `setInterval`, visually ticking down the hours, minutes, and seconds to the collision in real-time (e.g., `T-MINUS 04:12:35`).\n\n**Please refresh your browser window at http://localhost:3000** to load the changes. \nFirst, try double-clicking a satellite on the 3D globe to test the cinematic zoom. Then, open the CAM Drawer, switch the propulsion to **'Electric'**, slide it to 1.5 m/s, and watch the ticking countdown and updated fuel costs!","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Usage**\n\nI'm now focusing on tool selection, understanding that choosing the right tool is key. My aim is to leverage the most specialized tool available for each specific task to avoid unnecessary complexity. The broader tools will be considered only when a specific tool does not exist. I'm carefully analyzing the available tools to ensure I pick the most efficient one.\n\n\n**Refining Tool Choice**\n\nI'm now implementing the rules about tool usage, specifically avoiding `cat` for file creation and `grep` within bash. I'm prioritizing specialized tools (like `grep_search`) over more general options. I'm focusing on pre-planning the tools needed for any given task, using the rules as my foundation, and making sure my tool selection follows the guidance provided. I will only select tools when I know they can be successfully used.\n\n\n"}
{"step_index":231,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-23T19:47:41Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\n\"The 3D globe is glitching when I try to move it! The auto-rotate is fighting my mouse, the trackpad inertia is too slippery, and the camera is still clipping (blacking out) when zooming in. Let's make this feel like a butter-smooth 120Hz native app.\n\nPlease update app.js with the following fixes:\n\n1. Butter-Smooth 120Hz Controls:\n\nSet viewer.targetFrameRate = 120; to uncap the rendering speed.\n\nFix the slippery trackpad by disabling inertia. Set viewer.scene.screenSpaceCameraController.inertiaSpin = 0;, inertiaTranslate = 0;, and inertiaZoom = 0;.\n\n2. Smart Auto-Rotate (Stop Glitching):\n\nDo not run rotateRight() blindly on every tick. Add a boolean flag let isInteracting = false;.\n\nAdd event listeners to the canvas for mousedown, pointerdown, and wheel to set isInteracting = true. Set it back to false on mouseup and pointerup.\n\nIn the onTick event, only call viewer.scene.camera.rotateRight(0.0005) if !isInteracting and !viewer.trackedEntity.\n\n3. Fix Camera Clipping (Black Screen):\n\nPrevent clipping through the Earth by setting viewer.scene.screenSpaceCameraController.minimumZoomDistance = 50000; (50 km).\n\nWhen double-clicking an entity, use viewer.flyTo(entity, { offset: new Cesium.HeadingPitchRange(0, -Cesium.Math.toRadians(35), 100000) }) so it stops safely 100 km away, looking down.\n\nAction: Write the JS fixes. Once reloaded, open the 3D tab, drag the globe to confirm it stops spinning wildly when you let go, double click an object to confirm it zooms safely without blacking out, and take a screenshot.\"\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-24T01:17:41+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
