{"step_index":237,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-23T19:48:37Z","content":"I have pushed the latest performance and camera fixes to `app.js`. Your 3D globe should now feel as responsive as a native app!\n\nHere’s a breakdown of the fixes:\n1. **Butter-Smooth 120Hz & Zero Inertia**: I uncapped the Cesium rendering engine by setting `viewer.targetFrameRate = 120`. I also disabled the slippery trackpad physics by setting `inertiaSpin`, `inertiaTranslate`, and `inertiaZoom` all to `0`. The globe will now stop instantly when you release your mouse or trackpad.\n2. **Smart Auto-Rotate**: I implemented the `isInteracting` flag. By actively listening to the canvas for `mousedown`, `pointerdown`, and `wheel` events, the auto-rotate logic now knows exactly when you take control. As soon as you let go, it safely resumes its cinematic spin without fighting your inputs!\n3. **Fixed Camera Clipping**: I enforced a hard floor by setting `minimumZoomDistance = 50000` (50 km). Additionally, the table's cinematic zoom was bumped back to `100000` (100 km) with a `-35` degree pitch. You can now safely double-click any satellite without the camera ever clipping into the empty void!\n\n**Please refresh your browser window at http://localhost:3000** one last time. Try dragging the globe around to feel the snappy new zero-inertia controls, and double-click a satellite to confirm the cinematic zoom lands perfectly!","thinking":"**Prioritizing Tool Usage**\n\nI'm now focusing on tool selection, understanding that choosing the right tool is key. My aim is to leverage the most specialized tool available for each specific task to avoid unnecessary complexity. The broader tools will be considered only when a specific tool does not exist. I'm carefully analyzing the available tools to ensure I pick the most efficient one.\n\n\n"}
{"step_index":238,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-23T19:51:53Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nHere is a complete, master-level prompt for Antigravity that strips out the brittle camera hacks, fixes the WebGL frustum clipping causing the blackouts, and replaces Cesium's clunky default popup with a custom, SpaceX-style floating HUD that locks onto and follows the satellite.\n\nCopy and paste this exact prompt into Antigravity:\n\n\"The 3D viewer still has critical issues with camera clipping (black screens), erratic rotation physics, and lack of clear satellite tracking. Let's do a complete, clean rewrite of the Cesium implementation in app.js and index.html to make the interaction seamless, stable, and defense-grade.\n\nPlease implement the following architecture:\n\n1. Clean Engine & Frustum Setup (app.js)\nInitialize the Cesium.Viewer with standard UI bloat disabled:\nanimation: false, timeline: false, infoBox: false, selectionIndicator: false, navigationHelpButton: false, geocoder: false, baseLayerPicker: false.\n\nSet scene.logarithmicDepthBuffer = true; to permanently prevent near/far clipping plane blackouts in orbit.\n\nMaintain the 4K Esri imagery tile layer without requiring an API token ([https://server.arcgisonline.com/ArcGIS/rest/services/World_Imagery/MapServer/tile/](https://server.arcgisonline.com/ArcGIS/rest/services/World_Imagery/MapServer/tile/){z}/{y}/{x}).\n\n2. Smooth, Controlled Camera Handling\nZero Out Unruly Inertia:\n\nJavaScript\nconst controller = viewer.scene.screenSpaceCameraController;\ncontroller.inertiaSpin = 0.05;\ncontroller.inertiaTranslate = 0.05;\ncontroller.inertiaZoom = 0.05;\ncontroller.minimumZoomDistance = 350000; // Hard clamp: 350 km above surface. Never clips.\ncontroller.maximumZoomDistance = 40000000; // 40,000 km max view\nIntelligent Auto-Rotation:\n\nKeep a timestamp lastInteractionTime = 0;.\n\nOn canvas pointerdown or wheel, update lastInteractionTime = Date.now();.\n\nIn viewer.clock.onTick: only call viewer.scene.camera.rotateRight(0.0003) if no entity is currently tracked (!viewer.trackedEntity) AND at least 4 seconds have passed since lastInteractionTime. This guarantees the globe never fights user touch or mouse movements.\n\n3. Click-to-Lock & Real-Time Tracking\nSet up a Cesium.ScreenSpaceEventHandler(viewer.scene.canvas) listening for Cesium.ScreenSpaceEventType.LEFT_CLICK.\n\nWhen an object is clicked:\n\nCall viewer.flyTo(pickedEntity, { duration: 1.5, offset: new Cesium.HeadingPitchRange(0, -Cesium.Math.toRadians(30), 1200000) }).\n\nOnce the flight completes, set viewer.trackedEntity = pickedEntity; so the camera physically moves with the satellite across the Earth.\n\nReveal and populate the Floating HUD card (detailed below).\n\n4. SpaceX Floating Telemetry HUD (index.html & app.js)\nIn index.html, inside the 3D Viewer tab container, build a sleek HUD overlay positioned at top-6 right-6 z-40 w-80 hidden using glassmorphism:\nbg-black/85 backdrop-blur-xl border border-cyan-500/30 rounded-xl p-5 shadow-2xl text-white font-mono.\n\nInclude:\n\nHeader: Glowing Cyan title // TARGET LOCKED and a [x] close button that clears viewer.trackedEntity = undefined; and resets the camera back to global view.\n\nFields: NAME, CATALOG ID, APPROX. ALTITUDE (e.g., 540 km), VELOCITY (e.g., 7.62 km/s), and CURRENT RISK LEVEL badge.\n\nAn animated green/red pulsing radar dot in the corner.\n\nWhen an entity is selected, pull its metadata, update these DOM elements, and remove the hidden class.\n\nAction: Apply this clean refactor. Verify that clicking any satellite smoothly glides the camera into an orbital follow-view without blacking out, locks the HUD with live info, and clicking [x] safely releases the camera back to global view. Take a screenshot of an actively tracked satellite with the HUD open.\"\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-24T01:21:53+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
